-- title:
--   Moduli reading of a supersingular stalk with level-automorphism dictionary
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. Fixed are a prime $q$, a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$; a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $q\ell$, elements $\zeta, \xi \in L$ with $\zeta$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, $\xi$ a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity, and $\zeta = \xi^{\ell}$; a subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ which, by the hypothesis `hH₁`, is the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$ coming from $\ell \mid q^2M'$; thus $H_1$ consists of the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$ and modulo $\ell$. Further, $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ which, by `hK`, equals [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(L)$ generated by the image under the coefficient map [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ over $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   The base ring and the model. $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with a fraction-field structure $A \subseteq L$ such that $q$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$ in $L$, together with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \subseteq L \subseteq K$; $j \in K$ is an element whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-function, and $j \neq 0$; $\varpi \in A$ generates the maximal ideal of $A$. The scheme [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) is the pushout of the two $j$-chart models, whose finite chart is $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the subalgebra `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$. Given are a point $z$ of this model, the element $\varpi z$ of the stalk at $z$ obtained as the germ at $z$ of the global function on the model induced by $\varpi$ through the structure morphism to $\mathrm{Spec}\, A$, with the hypothesis `hz` that $\varpi z$ lies in the maximal ideal of that stalk, and a point $y$ of $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the finite chart algebra mapping to $z$ under the canonical morphism `ιFin`. The supersingularity hypothesis `hss` requires: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ whose kernel is the prime $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), that is, every Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with unit discriminant and $j$-invariant $\varphi(j)$ has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   Conclusion, first part: the unramified sub-base. There exist a type $A_0$ with a commutative ring structure making it a discrete valuation domain whose maximal ideal is generated by $q$ and whose residue field is finite, an $A_0$-algebra structure on $A$ with injective structure map and $A$ finite as an $A_0$-module, an element $\zeta_A \in A$ mapping to $\zeta$ in $L$ with $A_0[\zeta_A] = A$, and such that $\ell$ and $M'$ are units in $A_0$.
--
--   Conclusion, second part: the level data over $A_0$. There exist three functoriality hypotheses, each quantified over all $A_0$-algebras $T$, all Weierstrass curves $W$ over $T$ and all variable changes $C$: `hℓ` states that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma1Point W ℓ D`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma1Pow.html#L10) (the affine equation at $(x_P,y_P)$, vanishing of $\mathrm{pre}\Psi_\ell$ at $x_P$, and $x_Q = x_P$, $y_Q = y_P$) is preserved by passing to $C \bullet W$ and the transformed level datum $D.\mathrm{variableChange}\,C$; `hM` states that [`ModularCurve.IsGamma0PowAt W p k h`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassGamma0Pow.html#L55) (the two-torsion kernel condition when $p^k = 2$, and otherwise the cyclic-kernel-generator condition of degree $\varphi(p^k)/2$) is preserved on replacing $h$ by `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h`; `hL` states that if $h$ divides [`ModularCurve.inLineMulPoly W ℓ n x`](def/ModularCurve_WeierstrassH1Pow.html#L18) then `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` divides `inLineMulPoly (C • W) ℓ n (u_C^{-2}(x - r_C))`. Further there exist $\mathcal{G}$, a family of relative group laws on the projective Weierstrass models over $A_0$-algebras for curves with unit discriminant, satisfying the chord-and-tangent condition `IsChordTangent` and the condition `IsOriginIdentity` that the identity section is the origin section in the origin chart; a transport structure $\mathcal{T}$ of Drinfeld pairs at level $q$ over $A_0$ satisfying `IsSectionTransport`; and the two realisation hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO`, asserting for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, every projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, every variable change $C$ and every $A_0$-algebra map $f$, the existence of graded ring homomorphisms of the projective model gradings, surjective on irrelevant ideals in the stated sense, realising respectively the variable change $C$ and the coefficient change $f$. With these, there exist a fine moduli package $P_0$ for the moduli datum `rigidDataH1Pow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯` — whose raw points over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ are a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant together with a family of $\Gamma_0(p^{v_p(M')})$-kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, a $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-point datum, and a Drinfeld $q$-basis pair, subject to the link condition that the $\ell$-component polynomial divides `inLineMulPoly W ℓ (ℓ ^ (M'.factorization ℓ - 1)) xP`, taken modulo variable changes — so that $P_0$ consists of a ring $P_0.B_0$ over $A_0$ and a point $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ representing the functor, and $P_0.B_0$ is of finite type over $A_0$.
--
--   Conclusion, third part: the local comparison. There exist a maximal ideal $\mathfrak{p}$ of $P_0.B_0$ containing the image of $q$ and supersingular in the same sense as above (for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : P_0.B_0 \to \Omega$ with kernel $\mathfrak{p}$, $\varphi(P_0.j_0) \in$ [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), where `P₀.j₀` is the distinguished element of $P_0.B_0$ attached to the package); a ring isomorphism $\beta$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ along its maximal ideal onto the $\mathfrak{p}$-adic completion of $P_0.B_0$; a ring homomorphism $\sigma_0 : A \to \widehat{(P_0.B_0)}_{\mathfrak{p}}$ restricting on $A_0$ to the structure map $A_0 \to P_0.B_0 \to \widehat{(P_0.B_0)}_{\mathfrak{p}}$; and proofs that [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18), the $q$-fold $q$-expansion rescaling of the $j$-series over $L$, lies in $K$ and that the resulting element of $K$ lies in the finite chart algebra. Writing $STK$ for the stalk at $z$, $CMP$ for its adic completion, $\mathrm{toC}$ for the completion map and $\mathrm{germY}$ for the ring homomorphism from the finite chart algebra to $STK$ obtained from the germ at $z$ on the open image of `ιFin` composed with the canonical identifications of sections over that image with the chart algebra, the following hold.
--
--   First, $\beta$ reads constants through $\sigma_0$: for every $a \in A$, the image under $\beta$ of the completion of the germ at $z$ of the global function induced by $a$ equals $\sigma_0(a)$.
--
--   Second, $\beta$ reads the $j$-series as the universal element: $\beta(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\mathrm{jqNModC}\,L\,q)))$ equals the image of $P_0.j_0$ in $\widehat{(P_0.B_0)}_{\mathfrak{p}}$.
--
--   Third, there exist a ring homomorphism $\theta_K : P_0.B_0 \to K$ whose values all lie in the finite chart algebra, such that: every element of the finite chart algebra is of the form $\theta_K(b)$; for every $b \in P_0.B_0$ one has $\beta(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\theta_K b)))$ equal to the image of $b$ in $\widehat{(P_0.B_0)}_{\mathfrak{p}}$; and the following dictionary for level automorphisms holds. Let $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lie in $\Gamma_0(M')$ and let $\tau$ be an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29), i.e. for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ on the group attached to $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g$, $p_g$ giving a nonzero series, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the image of $p_f/p_g$, and every ring embedding $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, the product of $\iota$ applied coefficientwise to the expansion of $\tau x$ with the $q$-expansion of $g \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$. Assume further a hypothesis $h_{\mathrm{pres}}$ that $\tau$ maps the finite chart algebra into itself, and that the induced endomorphism of the chart algebra is congruent to the identity modulo the prime $y$, i.e. $\tau(a) - a$ lies in $y$ for every $a$ in the chart algebra. Then there exists an automorphism $\rho_\gamma$ of the moduli problem attached to `rigidDataH1Pow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯` — a family of maps on the points over $A_0$-algebras, natural in the algebra and preserving the $j$-invariant — such that:
--
--   (i) $\rho_\gamma$ acts by $\gamma$-relabelling: for every field $T$ with an $A_0$-algebra structure, all raw points $x, x'$ over $T$ and every proof $h_\Delta$ that the discriminant of the curve of the Drinfeld-pair component of $x$ is a unit, if $x'$ and $x$ have the same curve, the same family of $\Gamma_0$-kernel polynomials, if the point of the $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-datum of $x'$ on the base change of the curve of $x$ to $T$ equals $\gamma_{00}$ times the corresponding point of $x$, if the $Q$-coordinates of the $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-datum of $x'$ coincide with its $P$-coordinates, and if the Drinfeld-pair component of $x'$ is [`ModularCurve.LevelRelabelling.RawDrinfeldPair.relabel 𝒢 γ`](def/ModularCurve_LevelRelabelling.html#L53) applied to that of $x$, then $\rho_\gamma$ sends the class of $x$ to the class of $x'$;
--
--   (ii) $\theta_K$ intertwines $\tau$ with the classifying map of the translated universal point: $\theta_K(P_0.\mathrm{classify}(\rho_\gamma.\mathrm{act}\,P_0.\mathrm{univ})(b)) = \tau(\theta_K(b))$ for every $b \in P_0.B_0$;
--
--   (iii) that classifying map is congruent to the identity modulo $\mathfrak{p}$: $P_0.\mathrm{classify}(\rho_\gamma.\mathrm{act}\,P_0.\mathrm{univ})(b) - b \in \mathfrak{p}$ for every $b$;
--
--   (iv) if $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$ and $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$, then the image of $\gamma_{11}$ in $\mathbb{Z}/\ell$ is different from $1$.
--
--   This is the moduli-theoretic reading of the completed local ring of the cyclotomic two-chart integral model of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ at a point lying under a supersingular point of the $j$-finite chart, in the auxiliary frame where the extra rigidity is provided by a guard prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$ rather than by full level $q\ell$; it records both the identification of the completed stalk with the completion of a fine moduli ring and the dictionary translating Galois automorphisms of $K$ into automorphisms of the moduli problem. It is used in the construction of the Drinfeld-basis adic data and Hasse-parameter description of that completed stalk.
